# VS Code REST Client: set credentials, then send requests in order.
# Point baseUrl at the Go API origin, without an /api suffix.
# Full API: set BIOMETRIC_AUTH_ENABLED=true and JWT_SIGNING_KEY (32+ bytes),
# then restart. Alternatively, run `go run ./cmd/api` from api-go.
# A 405 can mean the wrong method/origin or disabled biometric routes.
# File variables have TWO escaping layers: REST Client, then JSON.
# In credential variables, write a literal backslash as \\\\ and a quote as \\".
@baseUrl = http://localhost:7004
@baseUrl = https://piccis.gwjz.uk
@username = elia.lombardi
@password = uvtecwtmCtZ7/5sdnU8N_FvFzP-R6.jmc5dDAy9M,t\\\\FWyzctg9\\\\TTNBBhr2
@deviceId = rest-client-test-device
@deviceName = VS Code REST Client

# Valid P-256 sample public key for testing registration only.
# Its private key is not retained. For successful verification, replace this
# JSON string with your own public key BEFORE registering:
#   umask 077
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out /tmp/spending-auth-test-key.pem
#   openssl pkey -in /tmp/spending-auth-test-key.pem -pubout | jq -Rs .
# Double every backslash in the JSON output for REST Client's variable decoder,
# then paste the complete string, including quotes (newlines must be \\n).
# Keep the private key outside the repository; remove it after testing.
@publicKeyPem = "-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\\nMFkwEwYHKoZIzj0CAQYIKoZIzj0DAQcDQgAES6GWwJ+fNgBCX4F/vcutJ8cgNvSo\\nid/lQtulBjPqhufnI55Pa4qIKkx24WQrB0ETtUAOj3V64r87DB4iIu6APQ==\\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\\n"

# After requesting a challenge, replace this with its signature (see step 4).
@signatureB64 = REPLACE_WITH_STANDARD_BASE64_DER_SIGNATURE

### 1. Password login (200; supplies the registration Bearer token)
# @name passwordLogin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 2. Register device (201 new, 200 updated)
# Must use a password-login token, not a biometric token.
# @name registerDevice
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/register-device
Authorization: Bearer {{passwordLogin.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "device_id": "{{deviceId}}",
  "public_key_pem": {{publicKeyPem}},
  "device_name": "{{deviceName}}"
}

### 3. Request challenge (200; expires after 60 seconds)
# No Authorization header required.
# @name deviceChallenge
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/challenge
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "device_id": "{{deviceId}}"
}

### 4. Verify challenge (200; supplies a 15-minute biometric token)
# Requires registration with YOUR key, not the sample public key above.
# Copy the challenge response value into CHALLENGE below, then run:
#   printf '%s=' 'CHALLENGE' | tr '_-' '/+' | openssl base64 -d -A | openssl dgst -sha256 -sign /tmp/spending-auth-test-key.pem | openssl base64 -A
# Paste the output into signatureB64 above and send within 60 seconds.
# Each verification attempt consumes the challenge, even on failure.
# Request a new challenge and sign it again before retrying (410 on replay).
# Challenge/verification endpoints each allow 5 requests/minute per device/IP.
# @name biometricLogin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/verify-challenge
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "device_id": "{{deviceId}}",
  "signature_b64": "{{signatureB64}}"
}

### 5. Test the biometric token on a protected endpoint (200)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users?page=1&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{biometricLogin.response.body.$.access_token}}
Accept: application/json